Letting $x=1$, we get the series:

$\begin{align}
\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{4(1-5)^n}{n^2\cdot(-4)^n}
 &=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{4(-4)^n}{n^2\cdot(-4)^n} \\\\
 &=4\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{1}{n^2}
\end{align}$

By the $p$-series test, we know this series converges.

Letting $x=9$, we get the series:

$\begin{align}
\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{4(9-5)^n}{n^2\cdot(-4)^n}
 &=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{4(4)^n}{n^2\cdot(-4)^n} \\\\
 &=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{4(-1)^n(-4)^n}{n^2\cdot(-4)^n} \\\\
 &=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } (-1)^n\cdot \dfrac{4}{n^2}
\end{align}$

By the alternating series test, we know this series converges.
